          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Birth and Early Life</strong><br>\nAndrew Carnegie was born in 1835 in Dunfermline, Scotland. The poverty of his childhood influenced his later views on wealth and its distribution. His family emigrated to the United States when he was 13.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise in the Railroad Industry</strong><br>\nCarnegie's career began in the Pennsylvania Railroad Company, where he learned about management and investment. He invested in various ventures, including ironworks and railroad sleeping cars.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Steel Industry Success</strong><br>\nCarnegie Steel Company, established in the 1870s, utilized the Bessemer process of steel production, significantly lowering costs and outpacing competition, making Carnegie a titan in the steel industry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Homestead Strike</strong><br>\nIn 1892, the Homestead Strike at one of Carnegie's steel mills led to a violent clash between strikers and private security, highlighting the era's labor tensions and raising questions about Carnegie's business ethics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philanthropy and Giving Back</strong><br>\nCarnegie believed that the rich have a moral obligation to give away their wealth for the betterment of society. His philanthropy was wide-reaching, including funding for over 2,500 libraries, universities, and various cultural institutions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Gospel of Wealth</strong><br>\nHe published \"The Gospel of Wealth\" in 1889, articulating his view that the wealthy are trustees of their riches and should administer them for the public good.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
